Macrophage activation syndrome (MAS)
Rheumatology & Immunology
Unremitting fever, Paradoxical ESR drop, Massive hyperferritinemia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-028
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe clinical collapse in a patient with known rheumatologic disease, presenting with unremitting high fever, acute confusion, massive abdominal swelling, and spontaneous bleeding from the gums.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with known systemic JIA presenting with sudden high fever, altered mental status, and mucosal bleeding. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was absolutely critical to assess for a fulminant cytokine storm, evaluate neurological status, and expedite an immediate emergency hospital admission, as delaying until standard hours carried an imminent risk of fatal disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and multi-organ failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "She has systemic arthritis, but suddenly her fever spiked to 40 degrees, she doesn't know where she is, and her gums are bleeding constantly."
History of Present Illness: The spouse reports the sudden onset of an unremitting, high-grade fever (40.2 C) over the last 24 hours in this patient with known systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis (now an adult). The patient has experienced a rapid clinical deterioration. The spouse noted the rapid development of massive abdominal swelling today. Alarmingly, the patient has developed spontaneous mucosal bleeding (gingival bleeding and epistaxis) suggesting disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). In the last 4 hours, central nervous system dysfunction has emerged, including lethargy and acute confusion. Reports extreme, generalized weakness and icterus appearing in the eyes.
Functional Status: Bedbound, profoundly lethargic, and unable to converse coherently.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of hemorrhagic shock or fatal status epilepticus; spouse is terrified.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 mmHg | HR: 135 bpm | Temp: 40.1 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 93% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly toxic-appearing, actively bleeding from the gums, icteric, and exhibiting altered mental status.
Physical Exam: Neurological: GCS 12 (E3, V4, M5). Patient is lethargic, intermittently rousable but confused, and unable to follow complex commands. Abdomen: Massively distended. Hepatomegaly is profound (palpable 5cm below the costal margin), and massive splenomegaly is present, crossing the midline. Dermatological: Widespread petechiae and ecchymoses over the trunk and extremities. Marked scleral icterus indicative of severe hepatic dysfunction and rapid hemolysis. HEENT: Continuous, sluggish oozing from the gingival margins. Lymphatics: Generalized, bulky lymphadenopathy. Diagnostic tetrad heavily suggested: unremitting fever and a known rheumatologic background strongly predict a paradoxical abrupt drop in ESR (due to hypofibrinogenemia) and extreme, rapidly rising serum ferritin levels upon imminent hospital laboratory analysis.
Environmental Observations: Tissues soaked in blood beside the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: On baseline immunosuppression for JIA, which has completely failed to suppress this hyperacute event.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) - Status: Exacerbated (Fulminant crisis)
Differentials:

Severe Sepsis / Septic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While clinically identical in severity, the presence of massive splenomegaly, severe bleeding diathesis, and the known rheumatologic background make MAS the primary working diagnosis, though broad-spectrum antibiotics will still be required empirically in hospital.

Acute Liver Failure (Toxin-induced) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, unremitting fever and massive splenomegaly, which strongly point toward a systemic macrophage-driven process rather than pure hepatotoxicity.

Acute Leukemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyper-acute (24 hour) onset of this specific crisis in the direct context of an established autoinflammatory disease (systemic JIA), though bone marrow assessment is required.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Imminent mortality without intervention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the throes of Macrophage Activation Syndrome, a catastrophic, life-threatening complication of systemic rheumatologic disease. The clinical picture of unremitting high fever, massive hepatosplenomegaly, bleeding diathesis (DIC), and encephalopathy represents a fulminant cytokine storm and multi-organ failure. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to immediately recognize the severity of the shock state, document the neurological decline, and mandate an immediate Priority 1 ambulance transfer. Deferring care overnight would have undoubtedly resulted in death, unequivocally justifying the MBS 585-600 service provision.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is critically unstable and requires immediate 000 ambulance transfer to an Emergency Department with Intensive Care capabilities. The hospital must urgently evaluate for extreme hyperferritinemia, hypofibrinogenemia (causing a paradoxical ESR drop), and rapid-onset pancytopenia. Immediate, life-saving high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone), cyclosporine, or anakinra are required to shut down the macrophage hyperactivation, alongside aggressive blood product support.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict flat positioning: Keep the patient lying completely flat in bed without a pillow; directly explaining that due to her critically low blood pressure, keeping the head level with the heart is the only way to ensure the brain continues receiving oxygen, preventing a coma while awaiting paramedics. Bleeding control: Instruct the spouse to apply firm, continuous pressure with sterile gauze to the bleeding gums; executing this mechanical pressure actively minimizes blood loss and gives the spouse a critical task to manage the panic.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; ICU admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Intensive Care admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched (Priority 1). If the patient begins having a seizure, vomits blood, or completely stops breathing, the spouse must immediately place her in the recovery position (if breathing) or commence CPR (if not breathing) and update 000.

